1. What Baby Boomers Can Learn From Gen Z

Baby Boomers, generally born between 1946 and 1964, came of age during enormous economic, political, technological, and cultural transformations.

Many learned the value of hard work, homeownership, institutional participation, and long-term commitment. But some of the assumptions associated with their era deserve reconsideration.

Lesson 1: Hard work does not automatically guarantee success.

Many Boomers grew up with the expectation that working hard, obtaining a good education, and remaining loyal to an employer would provide a reasonable path toward financial stability.

Gen Z has entered a world in which that formula can be much less reliable.

Young adults face varying combinations of housing costs, student debt, insecure employment, and competition for desirable jobs. Their circumstances differ substantially by country, class, education, and family wealth.

Gen Z challenges the idea that economic hardship necessarily reflects an individual’s failure to work hard enough.

What Boomers can learn: Distinguish between personal responsibility and structural conditions.

Someone can work two jobs and still struggle to afford housing. Someone else can work fewer hours but benefit from inherited wealth, valuable assets, or family support.

Recognizing these differences does not diminish the value of hard work. It makes our understanding of economic success more realistic.

The relevant question is no longer simply, “How hard are you working?”

It is also, “What opportunities does your work actually provide?”

Lesson 2: Mental health deserves the same seriousness as physical health.

Previous generations often faced stronger social expectations to keep personal struggles private.

Some people were taught to endure distress without discussing it, seek help only as a last resort, or interpret emotional vulnerability as weakness.

Gen Z has helped normalize conversations about anxiety, depression, burnout, therapy, and emotional boundaries.

This openness is not universal among young people, and greater awareness does not automatically translate into better mental health outcomes. Nevertheless, it creates opportunities to recognize problems earlier.

What Boomers can learn: Resilience does not require emotional silence.

A person can be courageous and still need help. A parent can be strong and still apologize. A leader can be competent and still acknowledge uncertainty.

The ability to discuss suffering openly can strengthen families, workplaces, and communities.

Lesson 3: Tradition deserves examination, not automatic obedience.

Traditions preserve knowledge. They can also preserve outdated assumptions.

Gen Z is often willing to question established expectations surrounding marriage, gender roles, career choices, religion, and personal identity.

Some of these challenges are constructive; others deserve scrutiny themselves.

The important principle is that the age of an idea does not establish its truth.

What Boomers can learn: Ask why a tradition exists before demanding that younger people follow it.

If a tradition promotes solidarity, responsibility, wisdom, or community, there may be good reasons to preserve it.

If it perpetuates unnecessary suffering or inequality, changing it may be the more responsible choice.

Respecting one’s elders and questioning their assumptions are not mutually exclusive.

Lesson 4: Technology changes the conditions of everyday life.

Boomers witnessed the transition from landline telephones and physical media to personal computers, smartphones, and artificial intelligence.

Gen Z, by contrast, has largely grown up with digital communication already embedded in daily life.

For many young people, online communities are not merely entertainment. They are places to learn, organize, build businesses, develop identities, and maintain friendships.

What Boomers can learn: Digital culture is not simply a distraction from real life. It is increasingly one of the environments in which real life happens.

However, Gen Z also needs older generations’ perspective on privacy, information quality, human relationships, and the consequences of sharing personal information online.

The goal should be technological literacy rather than either blind enthusiasm or blanket rejection.

Lesson 5: Authority should be able to explain itself.

In traditional institutions, authority has often been justified by seniority, credentials, position, or custom.

Gen Z frequently expects institutions to explain their decisions and demonstrate that their rules are reasonable.

This can create tension when younger employees question managers or students challenge teachers.

But it can also expose unnecessary bureaucracy, inconsistent standards, and abuses of power.

What Boomers can learn: Experience earns respect, but good leadership also earns trust through accountability.

The strongest authority is not authority that fears questions. It is authority capable of answering them.

